                                                              "source":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                              "author":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                              "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/interest-rates-and-stock-prices-old.html",
                                                              "date":  "2026-09-10",
                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgDslAhzkZu_Oq6rigZLlCaVn-tfCOa1vu3ybROAgHuWdrNTjeWjdAqX6_6ZHMIDNlYg6MmxdA-NciCoeOAwGWdq2GoeHsO1qvCXbrDMAMDkl9uJ5b2cR0cMQAqoH-hEAjvSIA1y6Z3kqp31L-8KS0qvM7hdLfxbLCtu17Ke766hvMmqsGR26fCLqJtnaA/w400-h285/USTreasuryRates2026.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues that the relationship between rising interest rates and stock prices is more nuanced than the standard inverse correlation suggests. Higher rates raise discount rates, but simultaneously affect corporate cash flows through inflation, pricing power, and reinvestment decisions ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â meaning some companies benefit while others suffer. Despite a 57-basis-point rise in the 10-year Treasury during 2026, the S\u0026P 500 held up because analyst earnings estimates for 2026 and 2027 rose by more than 11%, offsetting the discount rate drag. The net outcome for equities depends entirely on whether earnings growth outpaces the cost-of-capital increase, which varies sharply by sector.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s framework cuts through the reflexive \u0027rates up, stocks down\u0027 narrative that dominates financial media. The 2026 data point ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â earnings estimates up 11% against a 57bp rate rise ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â is a clean real-world test of his thesis, and equities passing that test matters. Investors who anchor to the simple inverse relationship will keep being wrong at key inflection points; the earnings trajectory is the variable that actually drives outcomes.",
