                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rKQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s320/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran examines the structural incentive misalignment in venture capital, where VCs are rewarded for pricing companies on growth metrics and exiting before fundamental weaknesses surface. This bias toward scaling over profitability has intensified as abundant private capital allows companies to stay private longer and reach massive valuations before going public. The result is a class of aggressively scaled but financially fragile companies whose broken business models are only exposed post-IPO. The post argues this is not accidental but a feature of how VC economics are structured.",
                                                              "take":  "This is a damning but well-supported indictment of late-stage VC incentive design, and it has direct implications for how public market investors should price recent IPOs. The extended stay-private era has effectively let VCs offload business-model risk onto retail and institutional public market buyers. The actionable read: scrutinize cash-burn trajectory and unit economics of any high-growth IPO candidate rather than taking the last private valuation as an anchor.",
