                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://media.beehiiv.com/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,onerror=redirect/uploads/asset/file/edc330cd-79d0-4826-a3a5-bf12f41ad6a3/alex_kantrowitz.png?t=1785677394",
                                                              "summary":  "Brown argues that current hand-wringing over Big Tech capex overspending fundamentally misunderstands the demand curve ahead. The coming wave of autonomous AI agents, robots, and humanoid systems will require vastly more compute than today\u0027s infrastructure can supply, making today\u0027s hyperscaler buildouts look conservative in retrospect. He cites AWS revenue growth of 37%, Azure at 43%, and Google Cloud at 82% as evidence that demand is already outpacing the skeptics\u0027 models, with Anthropic tracking toward $90 billion ARR for the full year. Brown\u0027s thesis: view the AI infrastructure build as an inevitable multi-decade transformation, not a near-term ROI question.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown\u0027s \u0027forest not the trees\u0027 framing is the right lens for the capex debate, and the cloud growth numbers he cites make the bear case increasingly hard to hold. The underappreciated variable is the agentic layer: once AI agents begin orchestrating other agents at scale, compute demand could inflect in ways current models don\u0027t capture. For portfolio positioning, this argues for treating infrastructure overweights as structural rather than tactical.",
