                                                              "summary":  "Brown profiles Ron Baron, whose mutual funds have generated over $60 billion in investor returns since the early 1980s by maintaining concentrated, long-duration bets on visionary companies. The outsized performance is anchored by early and large positions in Tesla (purchased at $14/share in 2014) and SpaceX, held through extreme volatility and institutional skepticism. Baron\u0027s edge is fundamental research combined with a willingness to hold for decades rather than chasing short-term consensus, a discipline most institutional managers cannot maintain due to committee structures and benchmark constraints.",
                                                              "take":  "The Baron story is instructive less as a template and more as a mirror: most institutional frameworks structurally prevent the kind of concentrated, patient ownership that generated his returns. The Tesla and SpaceX positions weren\u0027t lucky ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â they were the output of a process that accepts multi-year underperformance as the price of asymmetric upside. For individual investors, the lesson is that volatility tolerance, not just stock selection, is the separating factor in long-run compounding.",
